\description{
Supervised ordination of samples from two groups: one predictive
component that separates the groups, and orthogonal components that
describe the rest of the table's variation, unrelated to the groups.
OPLS-DA (Trygg & Wold 2002) gives the same predictions as a one-component
\code{\link[=plsda]{plsda()}}, rotated so that the plot of the first two components shows the
group difference along the first axis alone. Gives the same results as
pyopls and \code{ropls::opls()}, computed straight from sparse input without
forming any dense table or \eqn{n \times n}{n x n} matrix.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

Let \eqn{X} be the normalized table, its features centred (and, with
\code{scale = TRUE}, scaled to unit variance), and \eqn{y} the indicator of the
second group (1 for its samples, 0 for the first group's), centred (and
scaled) the same way. The predictive weights are
\eqn{w = X^T y / |X^T y|}{w = X'y / |X'y|}, as for a one-component PLS.
O-PLS (Trygg & Wold 2002) then removes, one at a time, the variation of
\eqn{X} that is orthogonal to \eqn{y}. With \eqn{X_a} what is left of
\eqn{X} before orthogonal component \eqn{a}:
\itemize{
\item \eqn{t = X_a w} and \eqn{p = X_a^T t / |t|^2}{p = X_a't / |t|^2};
\item the orthogonal weights \eqn{w_o} are the part of \eqn{p} orthogonal to
\eqn{w}, \eqn{p - (w^T p) w}{p - (w'p) w}, scaled to unit length;
\item the orthogonal scores are \eqn{t_o = X_a w_o} and the loadings
\eqn{p_o = X_a^T t_o / |t_o|^2}{p_o = X_a't_o / |t_o|^2};
\item \eqn{X_{a+1} = X_a - t_o p_o^T}{X_(a+1) = X_a - t_o p_o'}.
}

The predictive component is fitted last, to the filtered table
\eqn{X_k}: scores \eqn{t_p = X_k w}, loadings
\eqn{p_p = X_k^T t_p / |t_p|^2}{p_p = X_k't_p / |t_p|^2}, and Y loading
\eqn{q = y^T t_p / |t_p|^2}{q = y't_p / |t_p|^2}. Its scores are those of
the one-component PLS of \eqn{y} on \eqn{X_k}, and \eqn{t_p q} are the
fitted values of \eqn{y}, the same as a one-component \code{\link[=plsda]{plsda()}}'s up to
scaling: \eqn{R^2Y = |t_p|^2 q^2 / |y|^2}. All the scores are
orthogonal, so the components' \code{eig}s add up to what they explain
together.

Orthogonal components stop early, with a warning, when \eqn{|w_o|} (before
scaling) falls to \eqn{\sqrt{\epsilon} \approx 1.5 \times
10^{-8}}{sqrt(eps) = 1.5e-8} times \eqn{|p|}: then what is left of the
table has no variation orthogonal to the groups, beyond rounding error.

VIP (Galindo-Prieto et al. 2014) is, for one predictive component,
\eqn{VIP_j = \sqrt{P} |p_{pj}| / |p_p|}{VIP_j = sqrt(P) |p_pj| / |p_p|}
for \eqn{P} features (constant ones included, when \code{scale} leaves them
out): each feature's share of the predictive loadings. The mean of the
squared VIPs is 1, so features with a VIP above 1 are often called
important.

The table is never centred in memory: products with \eqn{X_a} are
computed from the non-zero values alone, in parallel, as in \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, so
a sparse table is never made dense.

\strong{Two groups only}

OPLS-DA, as pyopls, \code{ropls} and most tools implement it, models one
response: here, membership of the second group. With more than two groups,
use \code{\link[=plsda]{plsda()}}.

\strong{Choosing \code{norm}}

For microbiome counts, \code{norm = 'clr'} (with a \code{pseudocount}) is the usual
choice: OPLS-DA then works on log-ratios, which suit compositional data.

\strong{Validate the separation}

OPLS-DA is supervised: its predictive component separates the groups even
when they don't differ (with more features than samples, almost always).
Don't read the plot, or a high \eqn{R^2Y}, as evidence of a difference.
Test it with a permutation test of the distances, such as \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}},
or estimate the error rate of classifying new samples by cross-validation
(\eqn{Q^2}, which \code{oplsda()} doesn't compute).

\strong{Matching pyopls and ropls}

\code{oplsda()} gives the same orthogonal scores, weights and loadings as
pyopls's \code{OPLS(n_orthogonal, scale = True)} (\code{T_ortho_}, \code{W_ortho_} and
\code{P_ortho_}), and the same predictive scores and loadings as scikit-learn's
\code{PLSRegression(1, scale = False)} fitted to pyopls's filtered table, up to
the signs of the components. It also gives the same scores, loadings,
\eqn{R^2X}, \eqn{R^2Y} and VIP as \code{ropls::opls(predI = 1, orthoI = n_orthogonal)}. pyopls's own \eqn{R^2X} (its \code{score()}) is the proportion
of variance left in the filtered table, which is a different measure:
\code{1 - sum(prop[-1])}.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{ropls::opls()} does OPLS-DA under another name; no other common package
has an \code{oplsda()}.

\strong{Component signs and reproducibility}

A component's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return mirror
images of the same component. \code{oplsda()} flips each component so that its
largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips its weights and
loadings (and the Y loading) with it. Results are identical from run to
run and for any number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last \code{display},
with the ordination's title for \code{plot()} and \code{print()}: the method's
name, and for the methods that take distances, the distance matrix's
title, such as "Principal Coordinates Analysis of Bray-Curtis
Dissimilarity" (see \link{ecodive_display}). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation. If
\code{norm = 'clr'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # OPLS-DA of two iris species, as if their measurements were counts.
    two    <- droplevels(subset(iris, Species != 'setosa'))
    counts <- round(two[, 1:4] * 10)
    ord    <- oplsda(counts, 'Species', two)
    plot(ord)
    attr(ord, 'r2x')
    attr(ord, 'r2y')
    attr(ord, 'vip')

    # CLR-transformed microbiome counts, with two orthogonal components.
    grp <- rep(c('a', 'b'), length.out = nrow(ex_counts))
    ord <- oplsda(ex_counts, grp, norm = 'clr', pseudocount = 1, n_orthogonal = 2)
    attr(ord, 'centroids')

    # Supervised: always validate the separation, e.g. with PERMANOVA.
    permanova(aitchison(ex_counts, pseudocount = 1), grp, permutations = 99)

}
